    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 102102\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n202 PM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances and \n  an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring strong and gusty winds to the region \n  tonight through early next week.\n\n* Substantially cooler than normal temperatures will stick around \n  through next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nTropical storm Rachel continues to move toward the northern coast of \nBaja with the latest track taking the remnants up the lower Colorado \nRiver Valley on Sunday morning and rapidly moving into southwest \nUtah by Sunday afternoon. Showers and thunderstorms will gradually \nincrease across San Bernardino, Mohave and Clark Counties this \nafternoon and evening. Showers and thunderstorms will expand to much \nover the forecast area overnight. Heavy rain, flash flooding, and \nstrong winds will be the primary threats with these storms. The \nlatest HREF indicates widespread 1 to 1.5 inches of rainfall over \nmuch of the area and isolated amounts in excess of 3 inches in the \nhigher terrain. Additionally, the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has \nhighlighted southeast San Bernardino and extreme southwest Mohave \nCounty overnight for a 2% tornado risk. The area expands on Sunday \nto include southern Nevada and the southern half of Mohave County. \nIf a tornado were to occur, it would be a short-lived spin up on the \nlower end of the EF-scale. The Flash Flood Watches for most of the \narea will remain in effect until early Monday morning. Precipitation \nwill linger through Monday across our eastern zones, and be \nfairly limited on Tuesday. \n\nThe second weather impact will be the wind with both direct and \nindirect impacts from Rachel. Several areas have been upgraded to \nHigh Wind Warnings for Sunday including all of Mohave County, much \nof Clark County (excluding the Las Vegas Valley), Lincoln County and \nthe far eastern portions of San Bernardino County primarily during \nthe daytime hours. Wind gusts of 45 to 55 mph will be common with \ndirect impacts from Rachel but drier air and strong winds aloft will \nwork their way down toward the surface over Mohave, Clark, and \neastern San Bernardino and Lincoln Counties with wind gusts of 60 to \n70 mph. Lastly, when the winds shift from a southerly to more \nsouthwesterly direction during the afternoon, enhanced downslope \nwinds are likely in the lee of the Spring Mountains in the \nnorthwest portions of Clark County.\n\nLastly, temperatures will fall dramatically and will be 20 to 30 \ndegrees cooler than they have been this past week. High temperatures \nin the Las Vegas Valley will only be around 70 degrees (plus or \nminus a couple of degrees) through mid week. There will be some \nwarming later in the week but temperatures will remain below their \nseasonal norms into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nsouth-southwest winds expected through the afternoon with speeds \ngenerally between 20 and 25 kts, though occasional gusts to 30 kts \nare possible, particularly near shower activity. Winds will \ngradually back to the south-southeast this evening and overnight. \nExpect vicinity showers through around 00Z, after which point, a 30 \npercent chance of shower formation exists directly over the \nterminal. SHRA becomes the prevailing weather around 06Z, with \nincreasing chances of embedded thunderstorms continuing through the \novernight period. With thunderstorm formation, expect locally heavy \nrainfall, gusty winds, and frequent lightning. South-southeast winds \nwill pick up around 14Z Sunday morning, with gust speeds around 30 \nkts... increasing to 35-40 kts as the day progresses with a veering \ndirection to the south-southwest. 40 percent chance of gusts over 40 \nkts on Sunday. CIGs will fall as low as 6 to 8 kft overnight with \noverhead shower and thunderstorm formation.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty south-southeast \nwinds will continue along the Colorado River Valley and Owens Valley \nthis afternoon... south-southwest in the Las Vegas Valley and at \nKDAG with speeds 20 to 30 kts. Shower activity will increase as the \nafternoon progresses, with showers shifting from vicinity to \noverhead reaching KEED and KIFP as soon as 20Z... KDAG at 06Z... and \nKLAS, KVGT, and KHND at 06Z. Greatest chances of embedded \nthunderstorms exist this evening and overnight, with locally heavy \nrainfall, gusty winds, frequent lightning, and CIGs down to 6 to 8 \nkft. South-southeast winds will pick up around 14Z Sunday morning, \nwith gust speeds peaking between 35 and 45 kts as the day \nprogresses.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Czyzyk \nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
